[
  {
    "file": "web/src/auth/AuthContext.tsx",
    "line": 55,
    "summary": "The effect that logs the user out on a failed whoami check fires for any query error, not just a 401 (expired/invalid token).",
    "failure_scenario": "User has a valid token but the API returns a transient 500 or the request fails due to a network blip while whoami is (re)fetched (e.g. on tab refocus). `whoami.isError` becomes true, the effect unconditionally calls `logout()`, wiping the valid token from localStorage and clearing the query cache — the user is bounced to the login screen even though their session was still good."
  },
  {
    "file": "web/src/screens/MissionDetailScreen.tsx",
    "line": 55,
    "summary": "approveMutation's onSettled only invalidates the `[\"mission\", missionId]` query, not the `[\"missions\"]` list query, unlike MissionCreateScreen which correctly invalidates the list on create.",
    "failure_scenario": "A Director approves a pending mission from the detail screen (status flips to 'approved' there). They click 'All missions' to go back within the default 15s staleTime window; MissionListScreen's cached `[\"missions\"]` data is still considered fresh, so it renders the mission with its old 'Pending approval' badge and the status-filter counts are wrong until the cache naturally goes stale or the page is reloaded."
  },
  {
    "file": "web/src/screens/MissionDetailScreen.tsx",
    "line": 73,
    "summary": "rejectMutation has the exact same missing-invalidation bug as approveMutation: only `[\"mission\", missionId]` is invalidated, never `[\"missions\"]`.",
    "failure_scenario": "A Director rejects a mission (status reverts to 'draft' in the detail view). Navigating back to the missions list within ~15s still shows the mission under 'Pending approval' in both the row and the status-filter chip counts, because the list query was never invalidated."
  },
  {
    "file": "web/src/screens/MyAssignmentsScreen.tsx",
    "line": 63,
    "summary": "respondMutation (accept/decline) only invalidates `[\"assignments\"]`, never the affected mission's `[\"mission\", missionId]` query, even though accepting increments that requirement's confirmed headcount.",
    "failure_scenario": "A crew member accepts a proposed assignment from My Assignments. A Director simultaneously viewing that mission's detail screen (FulfillmentBar / Requirements panel, sourced from the `[\"mission\", missionId]` query) keeps showing the pre-accept confirmed/headcount ratio for up to 15s (the default staleTime), i.e. the fulfillment bar under-reports confirmed crew even though the assignment is already confirmed server-side."
  },
  {
    "file": "web/src/screens/MyProfileScreen.tsx",
    "line": 108,
    "summary": "skillMutation's onSettled invalidates only the crew member's own `[\"crew\",\"profile\",userId]` query, never the `[\"crew\"]` roster query that Director/Lead's CrewListScreen uses for each member's `skill_count`.",
    "failure_scenario": "A crew member adds a new skill on My Profile. A Director viewing the Crew roster list (CrewListScreen, `skill_count` column) within the staleTime window still sees the old skill count for that person, understating their qualifications until the roster query happens to go stale/refetch."
  },
  {
    "file": "web/src/App.tsx",
    "line": 125,
    "summary": "Role routing is done by checking only `role === \"crew_member\"` and defaulting everything else to the privileged Director/Mission Lead app (with the New Mission button, Crew roster, Skills admin), instead of explicitly checking for the two known privileged roles.",
    "failure_scenario": "If `user.role` is ever missing/undefined (a malformed or partially-cached whoami response) or the backend introduces a new role value the frontend doesn't know about yet, the condition `user.role === \"crew_member\"` is false, so the user silently falls through to `DirectorLeadApp` and sees the full Missions/Crew/Skills UI — including the New Mission button added in R-9 — rather than being denied or shown a safe fallback. The comment above the check implies an exhaustive two-branch mapping, but the code is a fail-open default instead of an explicit allow-list."
  }
]

